{%- comment -%}
  quick-view.liquid — the buy box fetched into the quick view drawer.

  Never part of a template's sections list and never editor-addable (no
  presets below) — it exists purely as a Section Rendering API target,
  fetched as `{{ product.url }}?section_id=quick-view` from
  `<quick-view-trigger>` in global.js, the same pattern `main-product.liquid`
  already uses for its own price/low-stock refresh. Because the request
  carries a real product URL, `product` resolves normally even though this
  section is not on that product's template.

  Deliberately lean compared to `main-product.liquid`: one image, no gallery
  grid, no accordion, no trust row. This is a preview, not the PDP — the
  "View full details" link is the way out to everything this section leaves
  off.

  `data-quick-view` on the wrapper is read by `VariantPicker.onChange` in
  global.js: inside a quick view it skips the URL history update (this
  content did not navigate the page, and rewriting a collection page's URL
  with a product variant query string would break the back button) and
  swaps the single image directly instead of calling `<product-gallery>`,
  which assumes a full stacked grid that does not exist here.

  Params: none — always rendered against `product` from the request context.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign current = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign section_id = 'quick-view'
  assign quick_view_image = current.featured_image | default: product.featured_media
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  `.section` is kept (not just `.quick-view__content`) purely so
  `VariantPicker.onChange` in global.js — which scopes its price/gallery
  updates to `this.closest('.section')` — finds this wrapper rather than
  falling through to `document`. But `.section`'s own block padding is
  built for a full page, not a drawer that already has `.drawer__body`'s
  padding (base.css:962), so it is zeroed here rather than left to stack:
  the exact double-application §4 already warns against elsewhere, just
  arrived at from a drawer instead of a second component class.

  No `color-scheme-*` class here, for the same reason: this content is
  always fetched into quick-view-drawer.liquid's `.drawer__panel`, which
  already carries `color-scheme-surface` — the custom properties it sets
  cascade to every descendant on their own. Re-applying the class here
  used to re-declare `background-color` a second time on top of the
  panel's own, and `.drawer__panel`'s rule (base.css) reads `--c-surface`
  while the scheme class's own rule reads `--c-paper` — two different
  variables that happen to match for every other scheme but are the
  opposite of each other by design for "surface" (the one scheme whose
  whole point is being scheme-paper with those two swapped), which is
  what produced a visibly different white block starting exactly where
  this element's own background painted over its parent's.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div
  class="section quick-view__content"
  style="--section-padding-block-start: 0; --section-padding-block-end: 0; --section-padding-block-start-desktop: 0; --section-padding-block-end-desktop: 0;"
  data-quick-view
>
  <div class="quick-view__media media media--portrait" data-quick-view-media>
    {%- render 'image-fallback',
      image: quick_view_image,
      fallback: 'demo-product-1.webp',
      alt: product.title,
      sizes: '(min-width: 750px) 320px, 80vw',
      widths: '400, 600, 800',
      loading: 'lazy'
    -%}
  </div>

  <div class="quick-view__body">
    {%- render 'product-badges', product: product, variant: current -%}

    <h2 class="quick-view__title h4">
      <a href="{{ product.url }}">{{ product.title | escape }}</a>
    </h2>

    <div class="quick-view__price" data-price-target>
      {%- render 'price', product: product, variant: current -%}
    </div>

    <div data-low-stock>
      {%- render 'low-stock', variant: current -%}
    </div>

    {%- render 'variant-picker', product: product, section_id: section_id -%}

    <div class="product__buy" data-buy-block>
      {%- render 'buy-buttons', product: product, section_id: section_id, show_qty: false -%}
    </div>

    <a class="link-underline quick-view__details" href="{{ product.url }}">
      {{ 'products.product.view_details' | t }}
    </a>
  </div>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:sections.quick_view.name"
}
{% endschema %}
